diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:2:1: `@overide` is not an annotation
  @overide
  ^^^^^^^^
Did you mean `@override`?
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:4:5: `@banana` is not an annotation
      @banana
      ^^^^^^^
Emerald's annotations are `@override`, `@abstract`, and `@test`.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:7:5: a test must be a top-level function
      @test
      ^^^^^
Move this function outside the type, then keep `@test` on it.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:11:1: a struct cannot be abstract
  @abstract
  ^^^^^^^^^
Only a class can be `@abstract`, since only a class can be extended. Remove `@abstract`, or declare a class.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:14:1: `@override` belongs on a method or property of a class
  @override
  ^^^^^^^^^
Only a member of a class that extends another can replace one of its base class's members. Remove `@override` here.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:18:5: a stored field cannot be overridden
      @override
      ^^^^^^^^^
Only methods and properties can be replaced by a subclass. Remove `@override`, and give this field a name of its own.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:20:5: a constructor cannot be overridden or abstract
      @abstract
      ^^^^^^^^^
Constructors are not inherited. A subclass declares its own and calls `super(...)` first.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:24:5: a type-level function cannot be overridden or abstract
      @override
      ^^^^^^^^^
It belongs to its own type and is never inherited. Remove the annotation.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:30:5: a struct has nothing to override
      @override
      ^^^^^^^^^
Structs do not inherit, and this one adopts no traits. Remove the annotation, or adopt the trait with `with`.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:34:9: a struct has no base class, so there is no `super`
          super.g()
          ^^^^^
Structs do not inherit. Reach the struct's own members through `self`.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:40:24: `area` is abstract, so it has no body
      func area(): Float {
                         ^
Remove the body, and let each subclass supply one with `@override`. Or remove `@abstract` to keep this body.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:43:28: `perimeter` needs a body
      func perimeter(): Float
                             ^
Add its body in braces. A method that each subclass supplies instead is marked `@abstract`, in an `@abstract` class.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:45:9: a type-level member has no `super`
          super.make()
          ^^^^^
It belongs to the type, not to any one object, so there is no base class's version of it to reach.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:48:18: a class extends at most one class
  class C extends A, B {
                   ^
Emerald has single inheritance. Keep one base class.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:50:17: a class can only extend another class
  class D extends List[Int] {
                  ^^^^^^^^^
Name the base class, as in `class Dog extends Animal`.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:54:9: `E` has no base class, so there is no `super`
          super.f()
          ^^^^^
Give it one with `extends`, as in `class E extends Base`, or reach its own members through `self`.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:55:19: `super` is not a value on its own
          const x = super
                    ^^^^^
Reach the base class's version of a member, as in `super.speak()`, or call its constructor with `super(...)`. Use `self` for the object itself.
diagnostics/inheritance-syntax.em:59:5: `super` is only available inside a class that extends another
      super.h()
      ^^^^^
Inside a subclass's method, `super.name` reaches the base class's version of a member.
